WEBVTT

1
00:00:12.952 --> 00:00:18.425
At Ned's cry, everyone onboard the Abraham Lincoln rushed to get a look at the creature.

2
00:00:18.426 --> 00:00:21.452
Captain Farragut gave the order for the ship to stop.

3
00:00:21.453 --> 00:00:26.944
It was quite dark by then, so I wondered how Ned had been able to spot anything in the water.

4
00:00:26.945 --> 00:00:32.039
But he had not been mistaken because we could all see the object that he was pointing to.

5
00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:36.777
About twelve hundred feet away, the sea seemed to be glowing.

6
00:00:36.778 --> 00:00:43.277
The creature gave off the intense mysterious light that several ships' captains had mentioned in their reports.

7
00:00:43.278 --> 00:00:48.348
The immense oval of light condensed at the center into a blazing core.

8
00:00:48.349 --> 00:00:53.436
"Oh, that's only some phosphorescent sea animals," one officer said.

9
00:00:53.437 --> 00:01:02.087
"No, sir, certainly not," I replied. "No sea animal ever produced such a powerful light; it's almost like an electric light.

10
00:01:02.088 --> 00:01:06.420
Besides, that creature is headed straight for us!"

11
00:01:06.421 --> 00:01:08.825
A cry of alarm went up from the ship.

12
00:01:08.826 --> 00:01:13.420
"Silence!" roared Captain Farragut. "Reverse the engines."

13
00:01:13.421 --> 00:01:21.499
Our ship moved away from the burning light, but the supernatural animal came after us at twice our speed. Everyone gasped.

14
00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:26.571
More surprised than fearful, we stood there unable to speak or move.

15
00:01:26.572 --> 00:01:34.723
The animal gained on us and circled the ship. Then it moved two or three miles away, trailing a glowing wake.

16
00:01:34.724 --> 00:01:40.547
Suddenly the creature rushed toward the Abraham Lincoln at an alarming rate of speed.

17
00:01:40.548 --> 00:01:45.389
It stopped about twenty feet from our hull and the light died out.

18
00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:50.801
The animal reappeared on the other side of the vessel as if it had slid under our hull.

19
00:01:50.802 --> 00:01:59.118
At any moment a fatal collision might have occurred. But instead of attacking the creature, we fled—a maneuver that amazed me.

20
00:01:59.119 --> 00:02:06.785
Usually Captain Farragut displayed no emotion, but at that point he looked completely astonished.

21
00:02:06.786 --> 00:02:15.206
"Professor Aronnax, I don't know what kind of formidable creature I'm dealing with," he said as if he sensed that I was questioning his actions.

22
00:02:15.207 --> 00:02:21.540
"I won't risk my ship in this darkness. Besides, how can we attack this unknown thing when we can't see it?

23
00:02:21.541 --> 00:02:27.857
How can we defend ourselves from it? We must wait for daylight to attack this extraordinary narwhal."

24
00:02:27.858 --> 00:02:35.174
The crew members of the Abraham Lincoln were on their feet all night. No one thought of sleep even though the ship slowed down.

25
00:02:35.175 --> 00:02:42.928
Imitating the warship, the creature let the waves rock it, but it seemed oddly determined not to leave the scene.

26
00:02:42.929 --> 00:02:50.587
Toward midnight, however, it disappeared, seeming to have died out like some large glowworm. Had it fled?

27
00:02:50.588 --> 00:02:58.571
At about one o'clock in the morning, we heard a deafening whistle like that produced by water rushing with great force.

28
00:02:58.572 --> 00:03:05.730
The captain, Ned, and I were on the small deck at the stern of the ship, eagerly peering through the darkness.

29
00:03:05.731 --> 00:03:11.587
"Ned Land," said Captain Farragut, "you've often heard the roaring of whales, haven't you?"

30
00:03:11.588 --> 00:03:17.476
"Yes, sir, but never a whale like this. If only I could get close enough to harpoon it."

31
00:03:17.477 --> 00:03:23.468
"But to approach it, you'd need a small boat," said the captain, "and that would risk the lives of my men."

32
00:03:23.469 --> 00:03:26.817
"And my life too," Ned said quietly.

33
00:03:26.818 --> 00:03:31.643
Toward two o'clock in the morning, the bright light reappeared about five miles away.

34
00:03:31.644 --> 00:03:41.135
Despite the distance and the noise of the wind and the sea, we distinctly heard the loud sounds as the animal beat the surface of the water with its tail.

35
00:03:41.136 --> 00:03:44.468
You could even hear its panting breath.

36
00:03:44.469 --> 00:03:53.539
We were on the alert and prepared for combat. While one of the officers loaded weapons, Ned contented himself with sharpening his harpoon.

37
00:03:53.540 --> 00:04:00.698
At six o'clock, day began to break; with the first glimmer of daylight, the beast's bright light disappeared.

38
00:04:00.699 --> 00:04:07.603
At seven o'clock, a very thick fog obscured our view, and the best telescopes could not penetrate it.

39
00:04:07.604 --> 00:04:11.436
That caused much disappointment and anger onboard the ship.

40
00:04:11.437 --> 00:04:19.349
I started to climb one of the masts; some of the officers were already above me. At eight o'clock, the fog began to lift.

41
00:04:19.350 --> 00:04:23.603
Suddenly Ned cried out, "Look! There it is!"

42
00:04:23.604 --> 00:04:32.095
Everyone turned toward where he pointed. A mile and a half from the ship, a long black body emerged a yard above the waves.

43
00:04:32.096 --> 00:04:35.920
The ship approached the beast and I examined it thoroughly.

44
00:04:35.921 --> 00:04:43.492
Prior reports had exaggerated its size; I estimated it to be no more than two hundred and fifty feet in length.

45
00:04:43.493 --> 00:04:52.313
While I observed the creature, it ejected two plumes of steam and water that reached a height of one hundred and twenty feet.

46
00:04:52.314 --> 00:04:54.651
So that was how it breathed!

47
00:04:54.652 --> 00:05:01.310
I concluded that it must be a sea mammal of some kind, although one that was electric, like an electric eel.

48
00:05:01.311 --> 00:05:04.556
At last the moment for combat had arrived.

49
00:05:04.557 --> 00:05:09.302
Captain Farragut gave his orders, and the Abraham Lincoln went straight at the animal.

50
00:05:09.303 --> 00:05:12.905
The beast allowed the ship to come within three hundred feet.

51
00:05:12.906 --> 00:05:18.119
Then as if refusing to dive, it made a little turn and stopped.

52
00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:22.722
We pursued the creature like this for almost an hour without gaining a yard on it.

53
00:05:22.723 --> 00:05:29.452
"Well, Mr. Land," said the captain at last, "do you advise me to send out the small boats to capture the beast?"

54
00:05:29.453 --> 00:05:36.698
"No, sir, because we won't take this beast easily," replied Ned. "Increase our speed if you can, sir.

55
00:05:36.699 --> 00:05:40.690
I'll stand at the bow and throw my harpoon if I get the chance."

56
00:05:40.691 --> 00:05:46.944
The Abraham Lincoln sped up to eighteen and a half miles per hour, but the animal swam at that speed too.

57
00:05:46.945 --> 00:05:52.198
For a whole hour, the ship kept up this pace without gaining on the beast.

58
00:05:52.199 --> 00:05:57.357
We increased our speed to above nineteen miles an hour, and so did the creature.

59
00:05:57.358 --> 00:06:02.428
"We will catch it!" cried Ned as the animal let us get a little closer.

60
00:06:02.429 --> 00:06:10.595
But just as Ned was about to throw his harpoon, the animal slipped away at a speed that was no less than thirty miles an hour

61
00:06:10.596 --> 00:06:18.579
A cry of fury broke out from everyone. It was noon and we were no closer to catching the beast than we had been at eight o'clock.

62
00:06:18.580 --> 00:06:23.079
Captain Farragut decided to fire the ship's cannons at the beast.

63
00:06:23.080 --> 00:06:27.579
One shot hit the animal but slid into the sea without hurting it.

64
00:06:27.580 --> 00:06:34.325
I wished the creature would wear itself out, but hours passed without its showing any signs of exhaustion.

65
00:06:34.326 --> 00:06:39.467
I don't know how far we traveled that day, but it must have been at least three hundred miles.

66
00:06:39.468 --> 00:06:42.309
Only darkness put an end to the chase.

67
00:06:42.310 --> 00:06:47.642
At a little before eleven o'clock, the electric light reappeared about three miles away.

68
00:06:47.643 --> 00:06:56.134
Rocking gently on the surface, the narwhal seemed to be sleeping. The ship approached it cautiously to avoid waking it.

69
00:06:56.135 --> 00:07:04.713
Ned was ready with his harpoon. No one dared to breathe; we were only about a hundred feet from the bright light.

70
00:07:04.714 --> 00:07:09.547
I saw Ned throw his harpoon and heard it strike a hard body.

71
00:07:09.548 --> 00:07:16.039
Suddenly the electric light went out, and two enormous gushes of water broke over the ship.

72
00:07:16.040 --> 00:07:21.937
Then the ship struck something and I was thrown over the railing and into the sea.
